# ============================================================================
# AICA - every runtime knob, in one file.
#
# Copy to .env (run.sh does this for you on a fresh machine). Both the Python
# app (backend/__init__.py) and run.sh itself read this file, so a value set
# here reaches the server, the model build and the Ollama process alike.
#
# PRECEDENCE: a variable already exported in your shell WINS over this file.
#   BACKEND_PORT=9000 ./run.sh    still overrides BACKEND_PORT below.
#
# Every line below is COMMENTED AT ITS SHIPPED DEFAULT except the handful under
# "must be set". Uncommenting a line and changing it is the whole workflow -
# nothing here needs a code edit. Numbers with a "measured" note next to them
# came off a stopwatch on this hardware; treat those as scars, not suggestions.
#
# The frontend (separate `frontend-aica-ui` branch) has its OWN .env for
# VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL / VITE_BACKEND_WS_TOKEN, pointing at this backend's
# /ws/audio. If you set AUDIO_WS_AUTH_TOKEN or CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS below, they
# must agree with what that file says.
# ============================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. MUST BE SET
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The conversation LLM: Ollama, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
#
# NEVER write "localhost" here. Measured on this stack: the identical
# /api/chat request takes 2.10s via localhost and 0.05s via 127.0.0.1 - name
# resolution tries an address nothing is listening on and waits out a timeout
# first. Ollama never sees it, so it is invisible in every server-side metric.
LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
LLM_API_KEY=ollama

# LLM_MODEL must name a model whose num_ctx is set EXPLICITLY. Build it from
# the repo-root Modelfile (`ollama create aruvi-base -f Modelfile`), which pins
# it - or run `python -m backend.scripts.setup_model`, which builds it from the
# best base you actually have and says so if it fell back to a weaker one.
# Pointing this at a bare tag like `qwen3:4b-instruct-2507-q4_K_M` lets Ollama
# pick a VRAM-derived default (often 2048-4096) that silently truncates the
# system prompt before its language rules - the agent then answers in English
# and invents identifiers. See SETUP.md.
#
# run.sh reads this too, and pulls/builds exactly this name.
LLM_MODEL=aruvi-base

# TTS. "edge" = Microsoft neural voices: no API key, no download, CPU-only.
# "svara" selects the placeholder adapter, whose load() still raises.
#
# PRIVACY: "edge" sends each reply's TEXT to Microsoft to be synthesized. Fine
# for the fictional Aruvi test data, NOT for real patient speech - swap in a
# self-hosted engine before that. See SETUP.md.
TTS_ENGINE=edge

# Required for the gated ASR model (AI4Bharat IndicConformer). Without it the
# microphone path is unavailable, but /console's typed turns still exercise
# the full conversation -> LLM -> TTS chain. Get one at huggingface.co.
# HF_TOKEN=hf_your_token_here


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. SERVER - where it listens, what it logs, who may reach it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by run.sh. 127.0.0.1 keeps the server off the network; the console's
# microphone will NOT work over plain HTTP from another machine anyway
# (browsers require a secure context), so prefer an SSH tunnel over binding
# 0.0.0.0:   ssh -L 8000:127.0.0.1:8000 user@host
# BACKEND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# BACKEND_PORT=8000

# DEBUG surfaces the per-clause timing lines; WARNING quiets a production box.
# An unrecognised value falls back to INFO rather than refusing to start.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Log line format, a standard logging format string. The default is
# timestamped to milliseconds, because every latency question on this project
# was answered out of this log and the events being compared are tenths of a
# second apart. Point it at your log shipper's format if you have one.
# LOG_FORMAT=%(asctime)s.%(msecs)03d %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s

# Development only - uvicorn --reload. Leave blank in production: a reload
# drops every live call and re-pays the model load.
# RELOAD=

# Force a dependency reinstall on the next run.sh (normally it installs only
# what is actually missing).
# REINSTALL=

# Do not touch Ollama at all - it is remote, or managed by something else.
# Under this, run.sh also skips `ollama create`, because the model would be
# built on THIS machine where nothing would read it.
# SKIP_OLLAMA=

# Where run.sh looks for Ollama's HTTP API. 127.0.0.1, not localhost - see the
# 2.10s-vs-0.05s note above.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# Shared secret for the /ws/audio handshake, checked against the client's
# ?token= query param. A WebSocket client cannot set a custom header on the
# handshake, which is why it travels as a query param. BLANK MEANS AUTH IS
# OFF - do not deploy to a reachable network with this blank.
#   Generate one:  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# AUDIO_WS_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Browser origins allowed to call /api/health and /api/calls. The React
# dashboard is served from its OWN origin, so without this its fetches are
# blocked by the browser and the dashboard reads as "backend down". Blank
# means no cross-origin caller at all, which is right for a console-only
# deployment - /console is served BY this app and never involves CORS.
#
# Comma-separated. "*" works on a closed network but is not the default: it
# lets any page a staff browser happens to open read the call log, which is
# patient data.
#
# This does NOT gate /ws/audio - WebSocket handshakes are exempt from CORS in
# every browser. The socket's gate is AUDIO_WS_AUTH_TOKEN above. Setting one
# without the other leaves the audio path open.
# CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. LLM BEHAVIOUR AND LATENCY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 0.3 is the product default. RUN EVERY EVAL AT 0 - at 0.3 the same prompt
# scored 9/14, 13/14 and 9/14 in one sitting, noise wider than any change
# worth measuring.
# LLM_TEMPERATURE=0.3

# Reply cap. Part of the context budget below, not an independent knob: 300
# was lowered to 160 to stop the prompt overflowing num_ctx.
# LLM_MAX_TOKENS=160

# GPU OFFLOAD, as a layer count, applied when the model is BUILT - change it
# and rebuild (`python -m backend.scripts.setup_model`). Blank = let llama.cpp
# fit the model to whatever VRAM is free.
#
# LLM_NUM_GPU=99 forces every layer onto the card and measured 2.2x on a quiet
# one: 12.9 -> 31.3 tok/s, the largest latency win on this project. It is NOT
# the default because llama.cpp reserves ~1 GiB of free VRAM and normally
# recovers from missing that by offloading fewer layers - an explicit num_gpu
# takes the recovery away. Measured on this 4 GB card with an ordinary desktop
# running:
#
#     projected to use 2915 MiB vs 3344 MiB of free device memory
#     cannot meet free memory target of 1024 MiB, reduce by 595 MiB
#     failed to fit params: n_gpu_layers already set by user to 99, abort
#
# ...which is a 500 from Ollama on EVERY turn, not a slow turn. Take the 2.2x
# on a machine whose card is genuinely free - close the Electron apps first,
# then confirm `ollama ps` says 100% GPU - and leave it blank on a shared one.
# LLM_NUM_GPU=

# MUST MATCH the Modelfile's `PARAMETER num_ctx`. A test enforces it. This is
# what conversation.py derives its history budget from - raising it here
# without rebuilding the model means the budget is computed against a window
# that does not exist, and Ollama truncates the system prompt from the front,
# taking the language rules with it.
# LLM_NUM_CTX=6144

# Bound on transcript history, in messages. The CHEAP half of the bound - the
# character budget derived from LLM_NUM_CTX is the half that actually holds.
# Measured: 24 messages of the LONGEST turns this server has produced ran 1058
# tokens OVER num_ctx 6144, which is why the character budget exists. Raise
# this only after raising LLM_NUM_CTX and the Modelfile together.
# CONVERSATION_MAX_HISTORY_MESSAGES=24

# LATENCY: how many characters the FIRST clause of a turn may run to before it
# breaks at a word boundary. The first clause is the only one whose latency is
# not hidden behind audio already playing.
#
# Measured (HANDOFF.md Sec6b): 32 -> 24 -> 18 changed NOTHING - 2.38s/2.43s/
# 2.43s to first clause - because the model's opening sentence ends at a period
# long before this cap applies. At 12 it finally moved (2.37 -> 1.89s), and did
# it by cutting mid-phrase and dropping the honorific. Lower it with a
# stopwatch and a listen, or not at all.
# LLM_FIRST_CHUNK_MAX_CHARS=32


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. TTS - voice, pacing, throughput
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Blank picks the female voice mapped for TTS_LANGUAGE (Tamil ->
# ta-IN-PallaviNeural). Set it to override, e.g. ta-IN-ValluvarNeural for the
# male voice.
# TTS_VOICE=
# TTS_LANGUAGE=ta

# Speaking rate. Measured on a real reply: +0% = 6.12s of audio, +10% = 5.57s,
# +15% = 5.33s, +25% = 4.92s. Past ~+25% the Tamil starts to clip, and callers
# here are often elderly. This is the ONE latency lever that measurably worked.
# Must look like +N% or -N% exactly; anything else is refused at startup.
# TTS_RATE=+10%

# Edge pads every clip it returns, and that padding is the long gap after a
# full stop. It is trimmed down to one deliberate inter-clause pause.
# TTS_CLAUSE_PAUSE_SECONDS=0.08
# TTS_CLAUSE_LEAD_SECONDS=0.04

# Amplitude below which trailing audio counts as padding to trim.
# TTS_SILENCE_THRESHOLD=0.01

# How many clauses may be mid-synthesis at once. The audible gap between the
# agent's sentences used to be one whole synthesis round-trip (measured 849ms
# to 10.8s per clause against Microsoft). Starting the next clause while the
# current one is still in flight hides all but the first of those behind audio
# the caller is already hearing. Raising it queues further ahead of playback;
# real concurrency is still bounded by TTS_MAX_CONCURRENCY.
# TTS_SYNTH_LOOKAHEAD=3

# Bounded worker slots across all concurrent calls - not one global mutex.
# TTS_MAX_CONCURRENCY=4

# Per-clause synthesis timeout. Edge is a NETWORK call and links degrade; this
# is what stops a bad round-trip holding a turn open.
# TTS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# Only for TTS_ENGINE=svara, whose load() still raises.
# TTS_MODEL=svara-tts
# TTS_VOICE_REFERENCE_PATH=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. ASR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ASR is Tamil-only; English hospital words come back transliterated and
# backend/transcript_norm.py rewrites them over the hospital's vocabulary.
# ASR_LANGUAGE=ta

# "rnnt" (accurate, used for the final transcript) or "ctc" (fast, used for
# the interim partials).
# ASR_DECODING=rnnt

# Hard cap on one utterance: 1875 hops x 16ms = 30s. A turn is also ended by
# the VAD watchdog below long before this in normal use.
# ASR_MAX_UTTERANCE_FRAMES=1875

# Bounded worker slots across all concurrent calls.
# ASR_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2

# How often (in VAD hops) to run an interim CTC decode while an utterance is
# still open. 30 hops x 16ms = ~480ms between partial_transcript updates.
# Lower feels more responsive and costs repeated CTC decodes.
# ASR_PARTIAL_INTERVAL_FRAMES=30


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. VAD AND TURN-TAKING - the noise-resistance knobs
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TUNING. Both turn gates are on the TEN VAD FLAG only. Never
# add an energy/SNR gate behind them: it was tried and it rejected real speech
# on a quiet mic. Loudness may refuse to START a turn; it must never be able to
# END one. See HANDOFF.md Sec5.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Lower = catches softer onsets, but more false triggers on noise.
# VAD_THRESHOLD=0.35

# Consecutive 16ms hops before a turn OPENS (4 = 64ms). Measured over the 87
# real captured turns in call_events.db, 46 transcribed to 3 characters or
# fewer and 34 to the EMPTY STRING - 39% of what the VAD opened held no speech
# at all. Candidate audio is kept and prepended, so this never clips the first
# syllable.
# VAD_START_FRAMES=4

# Consecutive hops needed to RESTART the endpoint countdown. Raise if a noisy
# room still holds the microphone open after you stop talking. Resetting the
# countdown on a single hop is what let background noise hold it open forever.
# VAD_RESUME_FRAMES=3

# Audio kept from BEFORE the onset, so a confirmed turn never starts mid-word.
# VAD_PRE_ROLL_FRAMES=8

# Silence that ends a turn: 22 hops x 16ms = 352ms.
# VAD_ENDPOINT_SILENCE_FRAMES=22

# THE WATCHDOG. A turn ends when nothing LOUD has arrived for this long,
# whatever the VAD is flagging - 44 x 16ms = 704ms. Without it, residual echo
# plus the browser's automatic gain control keep the countdown restarting out
# of an empty room, and every turn after the first runs to the 30s cap.
#
# MUST EXCEED VAD_ENDPOINT_SILENCE_FRAMES - deliberately twice it. Equal would
# make a quiet syllable mean the same thing as silence, which is the reverted
# behaviour that cut turns off mid-word. settings.py refuses to start if the
# two are equal. Lower it toward 30 if turns still feel slow to close.
# VAD_QUIET_ENDPOINT_FRAMES=44

# LOUDNESS AT ONSET - only speech this loud may OPEN a turn, so a TV or a
# conversation across the room cannot start one.
#
#   SNR      multiple of the learned room noise floor. RAISE THIS FIRST if a
#            noisy room still opens turns. The noise floor learns only from
#            frames the VAD calls non-speech, so a talking caller can never
#            raise the bar against themselves.
#   MIN_RMS  absolute backstop, int16 RMS. Measured per 16ms frame of real
#            Tamil speech: voiced p10 931, p50 2624, peak 9804 - so 200 sits
#            ~4.6x below the quietest speech. It is a floor, not a filter.
#            Lower it if a soft-spoken caller is being ignored; raise it only
#            if the microphone is unusually hot.
# VAD_ONSET_SNR=3.0
# VAD_ONSET_MIN_RMS=200

# How fast the room's noise floor adapts.
# VAD_NOISE_EMA=0.05

# BARGE-IN: sustained speech needed to cut the agent off mid-sentence. Never
# gate this on one frame - one frame is a cough. The WHILE_AUDIBLE figure is
# higher because the agent's own voice is in the microphone at that moment.
# VAD_BARGE_IN_FRAMES=15
# VAD_BARGE_IN_FRAMES_WHILE_AUDIBLE=40


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. CONVERSATION AND PROMPT
#
# The server caches these at STARTUP. Restart it after editing any of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CONVERSATION_AGENT_NAME=Gayathri
# CONVERSATION_PROMPT_PATH=golden/main_prompt.txt
# CONVERSATION_RUNTIME_CORE_PATH=golden/runtime_core.txt
# CONVERSATION_EXEMPLARS_PATH=golden/flow_exemplars.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. PERSISTENCE - the call log
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SQLite file. Enough for a single-process deployment; swap for a real DB once
# this runs on more than one process.
# CALL_EVENTS_DB_PATH=call_events.db

# Fernet key for encrypting event payloads at rest. BLANK MEANS PLAINTEXT.
# Do not leave it blank once real patient PII/PHI is flowing through the store.
#   Generate:  python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
# CALL_EVENTS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. OLLAMA PROCESS ENVIRONMENT
#
# Ollama reads these from the environment of ITS OWN server process, so they
# only apply if they are set before it starts. run.sh exports these and starts
# Ollama itself, which is what makes them apply on a machine nobody configured
# by hand. If Ollama is ALREADY running, run.sh can only warn - restart it (or
# the tray app) for a change here to take effect, and verify with `ollama ps`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Together these buy full GPU offload on a 4GB card - measured 2.2x, the
# largest latency win on this project (12.9 -> 31.3 tok/s).
# OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1
# OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0

# -1 = never unload. The model used to unload after 5 minutes idle and the
# next call paid a full 3.6GB reload - measured as a 14.9s first clause
# against a normal 1.3-2.6s.
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=-1
